Count of orders by delivery status


Code
df_master_clean |&gt;
  group_by(delivered_status) |&gt;
  summarise(total_orders = n_distinct(order_id)) |&gt;
  mutate(pct = round(100 * total_orders / sum(total_orders), 1)) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(total_orders))


# A tibble: 3 × 3
  delivered_status total_orders   pct
  &lt;chr&gt;                   &lt;int&gt; &lt;dbl&gt;
1 early                   87782  91  
2 late                     7308   7.6
3 on time                  1380   1.4


Good news! Around 90% of the orders are delivered earlier than the expected time. This could also mean that Olist might intentionally show the ETA more to account for unexpected delays

Looking at number and pct of orders placed at different times of day


Code
df_master_clean |&gt;
  group_by(time_of_day) |&gt;
  summarise(
    total_orders = n_distinct(order_id),
    mean_cost    = round(mean(total_cost), 2),
    median_cost  = round(median(total_cost), 2)
  ) |&gt;
  mutate(pct_of_orders = round(100 * total_orders / sum(total_orders), 1)) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(total_orders))


# A tibble: 4 × 5
  time_of_day total_orders mean_cost median_cost pct_of_orders
  &lt;chr&gt;              &lt;int&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt;       &lt;dbl&gt;         &lt;dbl&gt;
1 afternoon          31219      142.        92.6          32.4
2 evening            23354      142.        93.0          24.2
3 morning            21591      138.        92.8          22.4
4 night              20306      136.        89.4          21  


We see that 1/3rd of the orders are placed in the afternoon. Orders placed in evening, morning and night time are split evenly Also, mean cost for orders placed in afternoon and evening are higher than orders placed in other times of the day
